    "content": "DISCHARGE SUMMARY\n\nPatient: 68-year-old male\n\nADMISSION DATE: 15/03/2024\nDISCHARGE DATE: 22/03/2024\n\nPRESENTING COMPLAINT\nAcute dyspnoea and chest pain\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENTING COMPLAINT\nPatient presented to A&E with severe acute dyspnoea and pleuritic chest pain. Symptoms started suddenly 6 hours prior to admission. Associated with tachypnoea and hypoxia (SpO2 88% on room air). ?PE was queried given clinical presentation.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY\n- Type 2 diabetes mellitus (2015)\n- Hypertension\n- Atrial fibrillation (paroxysmal)\n- Previous DVT (left leg, March 2020)\n- Chronic kidney disease stage 3\n- No history of MI or stroke\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n- Father: Myocardial infarction at age 52\n- Mother: Breast cancer\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n- Metformin 500mg BD\n- Ramipril 10mg OD\n- Rivaroxaban 20mg OD\n- Bisoprolol 5mg OD\n\nEXAMINATION ON ADMISSION\n- Alert and oriented\n- Tachypnoea (RR 28/min), tachycardia (HR 110bpm)\n- BP 145/85\n- SpO2 88% on room air, improved to 94% on 4L O2\n- Chest: reduced air entry right base, no wheeze\n- CVS: irregular rhythm, no murmurs\n- Abdomen: soft, non-tender\n- Calves: no pain or swelling\n\nINVESTIGATIONS\n- D-dimer: 2850 ng/ml (markedly elevated)\n- Troponin: normal\n- ECG: atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response\n- CXR: small right pleural effusion\n- CTPA: confirmed pulmonary embolism (bilateral subsegmental PE)\n\nDIAGNOSIS\n1. Acute pulmonary embolism (bilateral subsegmental)\n2. Type 1 respiratory failure\n3. Atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response\n4. Background T2DM, CKD3, hypertension\n\nMANAGEMENT\n- Therapeutic anticoagulation with rivaroxaban continued\n- High flow oxygen therapy initially, weaned to 2L by day 3\n- Rate control achieved with increased bisoprolol to 7.5mg OD\n- Monitored on telemetry\n\nCOMPLICATIONS\nNone\n\nPROGRESS\nPatient improved steadily. Oxygen saturations normalised by day 4. Dyspnoea resolved. Discharged on day 7 with improved exercise tolerance.\n\nDISCHARGE MEDICATIONS\n- Metformin 500mg BD\n- Ramipril 10mg OD\n- Rivaroxaban 20mg OD (continue for 6 months minimum)\n- Bisoprolol 7.5mg OD\n\nFOLLOW-UP\n- Anticoagulation clinic in 2 weeks\n- Repeat CTPA in 3 months\n- Haematology review to assess for thrombophilia\n\nDr James Smith\nConsultant Respiratory Medicine",
